1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,076
It started with a clip of two aircraft
sharing one callsign.

2
00:00:03,076 --> 00:00:06,717
That raised a question: can an agent
surface ambiguity, stale state, or

3
00:00:06,717 --> 00:00:09,434
a bad readback without becoming the safety
authority?

4
00:00:09,434 --> 00:00:13,126
This is TowerOps. It is a synthetic
research simulation, not air traffic

5
00:00:13,126 --> 00:00:13,997
control software.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,214
The rule is simple. The model proposes;
deterministic code decides.

7
00:00:19,214 --> 00:00:23,105
A proposal is bound to an exact hash of
the world.

8
00:00:23,105 --> 00:00:28,708
To take effect it must pass a freshness
check, a human approval fixture,

9
00:00:28,708 --> 00:00:31,120
and an acknowledgement fixture.

10
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:33,999
Only then does the simulation change.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,402
Here is the local explorer. Two fictional
aircraft are converging, and

12
00:00:40,402 --> 00:00:43,237
the engine predicts a conflict.

13
00:00:43,237 --> 00:00:49,547
The advisory sets FIXTURE-B's north-south
speed to two nautical miles

14
00:00:49,547 --> 00:00:56,315
a minute. Both fixtures arrive, the gates
pass, and the projected conflict

15
00:00:56,315 --> 00:01:03,449
goes from true to false. Scrub the slider
to compare the two constant-velocity

16
00:01:03,449 --> 00:01:03,997
paths.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:15,111
Every step is in a hash-chained audit log:
proposal, screen pass, approval,

18
00:01:15,111 --> 00:01:22,666
acknowledgement, and only then simulated
actuation.

19
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:27,999
Each event hashes the one before it.

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,348
Now the same scenario with the
acknowledgement withheld.

21
00:01:33,348 --> 00:01:39,937
Approval alone is not enough. The engine
rejects it with ack_missing,

22
00:01:39,937 --> 00:01:46,526
the world stays exactly as it was, and
there is no actuation event in

23
00:01:46,526 --> 00:01:53,211
the log. The other six cases fail the same
way: missing approval, late

24
00:01:53,211 --> 00:01:59,991
acknowledgement, stale observation,
changed world hash, wrong advisory,

25
00:01:59,991 --> 00:02:01,996
and expired proposal.

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,057
Any result can be exported and replayed.

27
00:02:06,057 --> 00:02:12,854
The backend re-runs the fixed inputs and
compares the whole export.

28
00:02:12,854 --> 00:02:17,216
Change one field and it reports a
mismatch.

29
00:02:17,216 --> 00:02:24,926
To be clear about what that proves: it is
reproducibility under this engine.

30
00:02:24,926 --> 00:02:29,998
It is not a signature, and it is not
authenticity.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,484
We checked this. Eight tests cover all
eight cases in every combination

32
00:02:36,484 --> 00:02:40,502
of fixture switches, and real HTTP
requests.

33
00:02:40,502 --> 00:02:46,894
A Chromium acceptance run exercises the
interface at desktop and phone

34
00:02:46,894 --> 00:02:49,999
sizes, including tampered replays.

35
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:57,938
TowerOps: the model proposes, the gate
decides, and everything is replayable.

36
00:02:57,938 --> 00:02:59,999
Synthetic data only.
